# TypeScript / Node.js Rules

## Type Safety
- strict: true in tsconfig — no exceptions
- Never use 'any' — use 'unknown' and narrow with type guards
- All function parameters and returns explicitly typed
- Zod for runtime validation of all webhook payloads from external platforms
- Enums for all fixed value sets — never raw strings
- Nullable values typed as T | null — never assume non-null

## OOP in TypeScript
- Single Responsibility: one class does one thing
- Dependency injection via constructor — never instantiate deps inside class
- Repository pattern: all DB access in repository classes only
- Service layer: all business logic in services, never in controllers
- Interfaces for all external dependencies (DB, Redis, Kafka, AI)

## Error Handling
- Try/catch on ALL async operations — never let promises reject silently
- Custom error classes per domain (PingProcessingError, TranscriptionError)
- Never throw raw strings — always throw Error instances
- Global exception filter in NestJS for unhandled errors

## Async Patterns
- async/await always — never raw .then().catch() chains
- Promise.all() for parallel operations — never sequential awaits
- Never block event loop — CPU-heavy work in worker threads

## External API calls (OpenAI, Deepgram, EnrollHere)
- Exponential backoff on rate limit (429) errors
- Retry max 3 times with: 1s → 2s → 4s delays
- Circuit breaker (opossum) on all external calls
- Log every external call: provider, duration, status, tenant_id
- Never call OpenAI/Deepgram in the synchronous request path

## Logging
- pino logger only — never console.log
- JSON output always
- Every log: trace_id, tenant_id, service, level, timestamp
- Never log PII (phone numbers, SSN, email)

## Health Checks
- GET /healthz → liveness
- GET /readyz → readiness (DB + Redis connected)

## Environment
- All secrets via process.env — validated at startup with Zod
- App crashes at startup if required env vars missing — fail fast
- .env.example committed to git — .env never committed